subsection.p:

          Here also, you can use a standard JavaBean as a property in your model, but changes to this bean 
          won't be reflected automatically in the view. 

          To automatically refresh the view with the latest data coming from the model and its sub-models, 
          you need to subclass all your models from org.scopemvc.model.basic.BasicBean, and follow the following pattern:

public class PersonModel extends BasicModel {
  public final static Selector PET_SELECTOR = Selector.fromString("pet");
  private PetModel pet;
  private PetModel getPet() { return pet; }

  private void setPet(PetModel value) throws ModelException {
    if (pet == value)
      return;
    // don't listen to the old child model
    unlistenOldSubmodel(PET_SELECTOR); 		
    pet = value;
    // parent model listens to the changes in the child model
    listenNewSubmodel(PET_SELECTOR);		
    fireModelChange(VALUE_CHANGE, PET_SELECTOR);
  }
}
public class PetModel extends BasicModel {
  public final static Selector NAME_SELECTOR = Selector.fromString("name");
  private String name;
  public String getName() { return name; }

  public void setName(String value) {
    name = value;
    this.fireModelChange(ModelChangeEvent.VALUE_CHANGED, NAME_SELECTOR);
  }
}

          With this configuration, you can have in your view:

Selector PET_NAME = Selector.fromString("pet.name");
STextField petNameField = new STextField();
petNameField.setSelector(PET_NAME);


          Changes to the name property in the PetModel will be immediately displayed in the petNameField.
